WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
A student plated water from three places.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.520
After two days, the tap water plate had

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.240
three colonies, the creek water plate had two

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.120
hundred and fourteen, and the rainwater tank plate

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.160
had none.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.520
Is the tank water safe, and is the

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.239
creek full of disease?

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.320
Neither follows from these plates.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.280
Let's find out.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.820
Contaminated water can look clean.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.380
One way to count microbes that can grow

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.540
in these conditions is to culture them on

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:28.180
agar.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.420
Water authorities test for E.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.500
coli, a gut bacterium that signals faecal contamination,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.740
so gut pathogens might be too.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.020
A school test is different: it counts every

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.060
microbe that grows on nutrient agar, so it

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.860
compares samples, but it can't show water is

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:46.500
safe.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.940
The aim is to compare how many microbes

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.780
grow from tap, creek and tank water.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.620
The independent variable is the water source.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.140
The dependent variable is colony forming units per

00:00:59.220 --> 00:00:59.940
millilitre.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.980
A colony forming unit is a cell, or

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.300
a clump of cells, that grows into one

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:05.860
colony.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.420
Keep the agar, volume, dilutions, and incubation time

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.940
and temperature the same.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.126
Each sample goes into its own sterile, labelled

00:01:16.207 --> 00:01:16.886
bottle.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:20.446
For food, mash one gram into nine millilitres

00:01:20.527 --> 00:01:21.646
of sterile water.

00:01:22.127 --> 00:01:25.726
Then use aseptic technique: methods that keep outside

00:01:25.807 --> 00:01:27.046
microbes out.

00:01:27.327 --> 00:01:30.206
Use sterile pipettes and spreaders, work beside a

00:01:30.247 --> 00:01:32.886
Bunsen flame, and lift each lid only a

00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:34.846
little, and only briefly.

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:38.946
A polluted sample can hold millions of microbes

00:01:39.027 --> 00:01:40.466
per millilitre.

00:01:40.507 --> 00:01:42.866
Plated straight, they'd merge into a film you

00:01:42.947 --> 00:01:44.306
can't count.

00:01:44.427 --> 00:01:46.546
So dilute in steps.

00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:49.186
One millilitre of sample into nine of sterile

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:52.146
water makes one in ten, written ten to

00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:53.586
the minus one.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:56.466
One millilitre of that into another nine makes

00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:58.786
ten to the minus two, and so on.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.320
Spread point one of a millilitre from each

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.320
dilution onto three plates.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.200
Label the base, because lids get swapped.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.080
Tape each lid with two short strips, so

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.480
it can't open but air gets in.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.000
Incubate the plates upside down, so condensation can't

00:02:16.053 --> 00:02:18.400
drip onto the agar, at twenty five to

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.440
thirty degrees for two days.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.080
Never thirty seven: body temperature would favour microbes

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.200
adapted to us.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.840
You need two controls.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.400
The negative control is a plate spread with

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.280
your sterile water, handled the same way.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.000
It should grow nothing.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.400
If it grows colonies, your method added microbes,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.640
and every count is in doubt.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.880
The positive control is spread with a known

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.320
harmless culture.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.640
It should grow, which shows the agar and

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:47.760
conditions work.

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:52.206
Here's one heavily contaminated sample's series.

00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:54.646
Ten to the minus one is a film.

00:02:55.127 --> 00:02:57.846
Ten to the minus two has colonies merging.

00:02:58.407 --> 00:03:00.486
Ten to the minus three averages a hundred

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.646
and fifty six, and ten to the minus

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:04.646
four only fourteen.

00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:07.046
Count the plate with about thirty to three

00:03:07.127 --> 00:03:08.166
hundred colonies.

00:03:08.647 --> 00:03:11.206
Above that, colonies merge and the count runs

00:03:11.287 --> 00:03:11.686
low.

00:03:12.087 --> 00:03:14.486
Below it, chance matters too much.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.180
Now scale back to the original sample.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.100
CFU per millilitre is the colony count, divided

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.900
by the volume plated times the dilution.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.860
Here, that's a hundred and fifty six, divided

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.020
by point one times ten to the minus

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.060
three.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.140
Point one times ten to the minus three

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.460
is ten to the minus four, so the

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.420
sample held about one point five six million

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.180
CFU per millilitre.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.160
So be careful what you claim.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.200
Colonies show microbes that grow on this agar

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.920
at this temperature, and most are harmless.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.320
You can't spot a pathogen by looking at

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.960
a colony, and viruses won't grow on agar

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.680
at all.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.160
So the creek carries more microbes that grow

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.320
here, not proven pathogens.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.840
And no colonies doesn't prove water is safe:

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.680
the plate tested only a tenth of a

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.560
millilitre.

00:04:06.767 --> 00:04:11.566
Validity: controls, aseptic technique and identical conditions

00:04:11.807 --> 00:04:14.446
mean the counts fairly compare microbes that grow

00:04:14.527 --> 00:04:15.326
on this agar.

00:04:15.887 --> 00:04:19.886
Reliability: three plates per dilution show whether repeats

00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:20.446
agree.

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:24.766
Accuracy: calibrated pipettes, plates in the thirty to

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:27.726
three hundred range, and marking each colony as

00:04:27.807 --> 00:04:28.446
you count.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:30.786
The risk assessment.

00:04:31.347 --> 00:04:34.546
Schools buy only risk group one cultures, the

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.946
lowest risk group in the Australian standard.

00:04:37.427 --> 00:04:40.386
But a sample could hold anything, and incubating

00:04:40.467 --> 00:04:41.346
multiplies it.

00:04:41.827 --> 00:04:44.466
So treat every grown plate as possibly holding

00:04:44.547 --> 00:04:47.426
pathogens: keep it taped shut, count through the

00:04:47.507 --> 00:04:49.386
lid, and wash your hands.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:52.546
Your teacher sterilises used plates in an autoclave

00:04:52.707 --> 00:04:53.666
before disposal.

00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:55.786
Your turn.

00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:58.466
A student diluted creek water to ten to

00:04:58.547 --> 00:05:01.106
the minus two, and she spread point one

00:05:01.147 --> 00:05:03.826
of a millilitre onto each of three plates.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.906
After two days at twenty eight degrees, the

00:05:06.947 --> 00:05:10.146
plates grew forty one, forty seven and fifty

00:05:10.227 --> 00:05:11.506
three colonies.

00:05:11.827 --> 00:05:14.306
Calculate the number of colony forming units per

00:05:14.387 --> 00:05:16.306
millilitre in the creek water.

00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:17.906
Pause here to answer.

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:31.133
First, the mean: a hundred and forty one

00:05:31.294 --> 00:05:33.613
over three is forty seven.

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.013
Point one times ten to the minus two

00:05:36.254 --> 00:05:38.573
is a thousandth of a millilitre.

00:05:38.694 --> 00:05:41.453
Forty seven divided by a thousandth is forty

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:44.973
seven thousand CFU per millilitre.

00:05:45.054 --> 00:05:48.173
Four thousand seven hundred is the classic slip:

00:05:48.254 --> 00:05:50.813
that divides by the dilution but forgets the

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:52.173
point one millilitre.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.600
A student spread one millilitre of undiluted creek

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.640
water onto one plate, incubated it at thirty

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.920
seven degrees, then opened it and wrote, "Too

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.680
many colonies to count.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.320
The creek water contains dangerous bacteria."

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.160
Identify two problems, and propose an improvement that

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.400
fixes each.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.120
Pause here to answer.

00:06:24.534 --> 00:06:29.493
One: undiluted, the colonies merged, so serially dilute

00:06:29.574 --> 00:06:31.573
and count the plate with about thirty to

00:06:31.654 --> 00:06:32.853
three hundred.

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:36.533
Two: thirty seven degrees favours microbes adapted to

00:06:36.614 --> 00:06:39.973
us, and opening it exposed her, so incubate

00:06:40.054 --> 00:06:42.693
at twenty five to thirty, taped and never

00:06:42.774 --> 00:06:43.813
opened.

00:06:43.894 --> 00:06:48.373
Her claim also overreaches: colonies don't show pathogens.

00:06:48.454 --> 00:06:51.733
Repeating the undiluted plate is the classic mistake:

00:06:51.814 --> 00:06:53.333
it still can't be counted.

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:59.493
So: sterile samples, serial dilutions, three plates each,

00:06:59.614 --> 00:07:02.613
both controls, and taped plates upside down at

00:07:02.694 --> 00:07:04.853
twenty five to thirty degrees.

00:07:04.934 --> 00:07:06.773
Count the plate with about thirty to three

00:07:06.854 --> 00:07:10.373
hundred, scale back to CFU per millilitre, and

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:14.013
claim culturable microbes, not pathogens.

00:07:14.454 --> 00:07:18.213
Next: how disease damages crops and farm animals.

00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:20.873
Here are the key notes.

00:07:21.114 --> 00:07:23.033
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:23.114 --> 00:07:23.913
to copy them.
